      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to connect strategy with execution. Yet many become unreliable lists of features, missed deadlines, and outdated assumptions. They fail not because planning is useless, but because organizations often expect roadmaps to provide certainty in an environment defined by uncertainty.\n\nThe first problem is that many roadmaps are not strategic. Instead of expressing customer problems, business outcomes, and important choices, they become inventories of stakeholder requests. Sales wants one feature, executives want another, and competitors inspire several more. The resulting roadmap may satisfy internal politics, but it offers no coherent explanation of how the product will create value.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they are treated as fixed commitments. Teams are asked to predict, months in advance, exactly what they will build and when they will deliver it. But product development involves discovery. Customer needs, technical constraints, market conditions, and regulatory requirements can change quickly. A roadmap that cannot adapt to new evidence forces teams either to build the wrong thing or to appear unreliable when plans change.\n\nAnother common failure is confusing outputs with outcomes. Shipping a dashboard or launching an integration is an output; improving retention, reducing customer effort, or increasing conversion is an outcome. Feature-based roadmaps encourage teams to measure progress by delivery rather than impact. This can create a productive-looking organization that releases frequently without solving meaningful problems.\n\nPoor prioritization makes matters worse. When every request is labeled important, teams become overloaded and fragmented. Frequent changes in direction increase context switching, create technical debt, and prevent initiatives from receiving enough attention to succeed. A roadmap without explicit trade-offs is not a roadmap—it is a wish list.\n\nMany roadmaps also ignore capacity and dependencies. They assume ideal staffing, underestimate maintenance work, and overlook research, testing, infrastructure, security, and coordination with other teams. Dates then become detached from operational reality. Once a few items slip, confidence in the entire plan collapses.\n\nFinally, roadmaps often fail as communication tools. Different audiences interpret them differently: executives see commitments, sales teams see promises, and product teams see hypotheses. Without clear language about confidence, time horizons, and decision criteria, the roadmap creates misalignment rather than clarity.\n\nEffective roadmaps are living strategic documents. They focus on problems and outcomes, distinguish near-term commitments from longer-term possibilities, and make priorities and trade-offs visible. They are reviewed as evidence changes and supported by measurable goals. The best roadmap does not pretend to predict the future. It helps an organization navigate it.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack ambition, but because they confuse a roadmap with a contract. A roadmap is supposed to be a strategic communication: where the product is going, why, and what questions need answering. In practice, many organizations treat it as a delivery calendar, filled with dates, features, and guarantees. That turns a tool for alignment into a source of stress, dishonesty, and dysfunction.\n\nThe first reason roadmaps fail is uncertainty. Product discovery, unlike manufacturing, is an iterative process of learning. Teams do not know in advance how long a feature will take, whether it will solve the user problem, or what technical constraints they will uncover. When a roadmap insists on precise timing, it forces people to commit to things they cannot reasonably predict. Estimates become politics: padded to protect the team or shortened to satisfy leadership. Either way, reality eventually exposes the gap, and trust in the roadmap erodes.\n\nA second failure mode is that roadmaps are too output-focused. They list features—“build search,” “add notifications,” “launch dashboard”—instead of outcomes or problems. This encourages teams to ship what was promised rather than to validate what matters. Features arrive, but metrics do not improve, customers remain confused, and the product grows heavier. A roadmap that names activities cannot tell you whether you are winning; it only tells you whether you are busy.\n\nRoadmaps also fail because they are made for the wrong audience. Many are designed to reassure executives, not to guide builders. As a result, they contain polished commitments but lack the messy details engineers and designers need: hypotheses, risks, experiments, dependencies, and trade-offs. If the people executing the work do not understand why an initiative is on the plan, they optimize for checkbox delivery rather than problem-solving. Conversely, when roadmaps are shared too literally with customers, they create contractual expectations that are nearly impossible to meet without damaging credibility.\n\nPerhaps the deepest issue is rigidity. A useful roadmap should adapt as learning accumulates. Yet many organizations treat change as failure. If priorities shift after a discovery interview, a prototype, or a competitor move, the revised plan is seen as a broken promise rather than evidence of good judgment. This produces a defensive culture where bad news is delayed, experiments are discouraged, and leadership mistakes a clean plan for competent management.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps fail because they are not truly strategic. They reflect the squeaky wheel’s demand, the last executive review, or the sales team’s largest deal. Without a clear thesis about which problems matter most, a roadmap becomes a backlog with dates. It may look detailed, but it cannot align people, guide trade-offs, or sustain focus.\n\nA better roadmap would be outcome-based, flexible, and honest about uncertainty. It would communicate direction and priorities, not fake certainty. It would be treated as a living tool for conversation, not a document that quietly predicts the future. Most roadmaps fail because they ask teams to promise what they can only discover.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
